Switch: Aurora's sqlite caches with a persistent rollback journal, no VACUUM

The game on the console is usually closed with HOME, which kills the process. With the
previous journal_mode=MEMORY a commit (or the VACUUM after the Dawn cache prune, which
rewrites the whole file) cut short by the kill left dawn_cache.db damaged: the next run
failed every lookup ("database disk image is malformed"). Mac kill -9 test with the
Switch's sqlite build options (switch/aurora/sqlite_kill_test.c): 113 of 150 kills damaged
the file with MEMORY, 0 of 150 with PERSIST, TRUNCATE or DELETE.

On Horizon the caches now use journal_mode=PERSIST (the -journal file is created once and
its header zeroed at each commit instead of the file being deleted; with the build's
exclusive locking it stays open, so the per-transaction create/stat/unlink of a DELETE
journal never happens), journal_size_limit=4 MiB and synchronous=OFF (what write() handed
the file system before a kill still reaches the card; a commit cut short is rolled back
from the journal at the next open). COS_SWITCH_SQLITE_JOURNAL (env.txt) picks persist
(default), truncate, delete or memory; a journal file that cannot be created falls back to
memory with one line. Each cache logs its journal mode once when it opens.

The Dawn cache prune no longer VACUUMs on Horizon: that rewrote tens of MiB on the SD card
(twice with a journal) for seconds with cache_mutex held, so every Dawn lookup on the
render worker waited; freed pages are reused by later inserts.

A cache that cannot be opened, for any reason, is removed and created once more from an
empty file (the shared patch native/patches/aurora/0007 does the removal and retry, for
damaged files only on other platforms). The sqlite build adds exclusive locking and
in-memory temp files, and its error log goes to the run log (switch/aurora/sqlite_horizon.c).

Switch only (switch/native/aurora/patches); not for upstream.

